\title[Hints compound by one orientation bit per field]{Hints Compound by One Orientation Bit per Field:\\ Conditional Hint Synergy, the Quadratic Bottleneck,\\ and the Legendre Symbol}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-09-24}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study how much information about a secret label $T$ can be recovered from the \emph{sum} $s=p+q$ and the \emph{gap} $d=q-p$ of a pair of factor residues $(p,q)$ when the \emph{product} $N=pq$ is already known. We define the conditional hint value of each dial, $I(T;N,s)-I(T;N)$ and $I(T;N,d)-I(T;N)$, the joint hint value $I(T;s,d)-I(T;N)$, and the \emph{hint synergy}, which is the joint value minus the two single-dial values.

We prove four groups of results. (i) Hint synergy has no sign: an explicit battery modulo $5$ has synergy exactly $-1$ bit, and one modulo $7$ has synergy exactly $+1$ bit. (ii) \emph{The One-Bit Law:} over residues modulo any odd prime, and for every population and label alphabet, hint synergy is at most $1-\max(\sumH,\gapH)\le 1$ bit. Equality holds exactly when both single hints vanish and the joint value is one bit. (iii) \emph{The Orientation-Bit-per-Field Law:} over a coefficient ring carrying a sign selector with $|F|$ values, synergy is at most $\log_2|F|$. Over a product of $k$ odd prime fields it is therefore at most $k$ bits, and a sixteen-sample battery over $\Z/7\times\Z/7$ attains $2$ bits exactly. (iv) \emph{The Legendre Law:} a cell $(N,s)$ of a prime field with nonzero discriminant $\Delta=s^2-4N$ hosts two distinct factorisations, and hence one orientation bit, if and only if $\leg{\Delta}{p}=1$. Batteries whose hinted sum view determines the gap, in particular batteries of square factorisations, cannot compound, and any positive orientation gain requires a nonzero-square discriminant.

As an application, an empirically reported synergy of $+1.40$ bits on a two-prime modulus is impossible over one prime field and realisable over two.
\end{abstract}

