\section{Introduction}

Two random variables $X,Y$ can each be independent of a target $T$ and still determine it jointly; the parity $T=X\oplus Y$ is the standard example. Conversely, two variables can each determine $T$, which makes them fully redundant. The interaction information $I(T;X,Y)-I(T;X)-I(T;Y)$ is accordingly not sign-definite.

This paper looks at a structured instance that comes from factoring. A secret pair $(p,q)$ of residues in a ring $R$ is given, and the product $N=pq$ is public. Two dials can be revealed: the sum $s=p+q$ and the gap $d=q-p$. A label $T$ attached to each sample is the target.

A large-scale experiment with a $36$-label battery over a two-prime modulus combined by the Chinese Remainder Theorem reported the plug-in mutual informations shown in Table~\ref{tab:exp}.
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
view & bits\\
\midrule
product view $N$ (hint-free) & $2.1314$\\
sum view alone & $0.6432$\\
gap view alone & $0.6496$\\
joint view $(s,d)$ & $4.5605$ (about $99.1\%$ of ceiling)\\
\textbf{joint hint value} $I(T;s,d)-I(T;N)$ & $\mathbf{+2.4291}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Reported experimental routing table.}\label{tab:exp}
\end{table}
The experiment set this joint hint value against a per-dial hint total of $+1.0288$ bits, and so reported a hint synergy of about $+1.40$ bits. It concluded that ``hints compound like capacities''. The ordering $I(T;N)<I(T;s,d)\le H(T)$ was confirmed.

We answer the underlying question exactly: how far can hints compound? Once a hint is measured as the \emph{conditional} gain on top of the always-available product, the synergy is bounded by the number of sign patterns of the coefficient ring. That is one bit per prime field. The bound is attained, and the orientation bit it counts is governed by the Legendre symbol. All values in Sections~\ref{sec:sharp} and~\ref{sec:legendre} are exact rational numbers of bits obtained from fibre counts.

\section{Definitions}

\subsection{Populations and plug-in information}
Let $\Omega$ be a finite nonempty population with the uniform measure. A \emph{statistic} is a function $f:\Omega\to\mathcal A$. Write $\cnt_f(a)=|f^{-1}(a)|$. The plug-in entropy in bits is
\[
H(f)=-\sum_a\frac{\cnt_f(a)}{|\Omega|}\log_2\frac{\cnt_f(a)}{|\Omega|},
\]
and for a label $T:\Omega\to\Lambda$ we put $I(T;f)=H(T)+H(f)-H(T,f)$. We use the standard facts:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item $0\le I(T;f)\le H(T)$, with equality on the right when $f$ determines $T$;
\item \emph{fibre invariance}: statistics with the same fibres have the same information;
\item \emph{monotonicity}: if $f$ refines $g$, then $I(T;g)\le I(T;f)$;
\item \emph{pairing bound}: $I(T;(f,g))\le I(T;f)+H(g)$;
\item \emph{cardinality bound}: if $g$ takes values in a finite set $F$, then $H(g)\le\log_2|F|$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Batteries and views}
Let $R$ be a commutative ring. A \emph{battery} over $R$ is a population $\Omega$ with a label $T:\Omega\to\Lambda$ and factor residues $P,Q:\Omega\to R$. Its views are the product $N=PQ$, the sum $s=P+Q$, the gap $d=Q-P$, the joint view $(s,d)$, and the hinted views $(N,s)$ and $(N,d)$. If $2\in R^\times$, then $(s,d)$ has the same fibres as $(P,Q)$ and $N=(s^2-d^2)/4$.

\begin{definition}[Conditional hints]
\[
\sumH=I(T;N,s)-I(T;N),\qquad \gapH=I(T;N,d)-I(T;N),
\]
\[
\hV=I(T;s,d)-I(T;N),\qquad \syn=\hV-\sumH-\gapH.
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Sign selectors}
\begin{definition}[Sign selector]
A sign selector for $R$ with values in $F$ is a map $\sigma:R\to F$ such that $a^2=b^2$ and $\sigma(a)=\sigma(b)$ imply $a=b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Lower-half selector]\label{lem:zsel}
For an odd prime $p$, the map $\sigma(a)=[\,2\bar a<p\,]$, where $\bar a\in\{0,\dots,p-1\}$ is the standard representative of $a$, is a sign selector for $\Z/p$ with two values.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In a field $a^2=b^2$ forces $a=\pm b$. If $a=-b\neq0$, then $\overline{-a}=p-\bar a$, and since $p$ is odd exactly one of $\bar a$ and $p-\bar a$ is below $p/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Selectors multiply]\label{lem:prod}
If $\sigma_i:R_i\to F_i$ are sign selectors, then the componentwise map is a sign selector for $\prod_iR_i$ with values in $\prod_iF_i$.
\end{lemma}

So $\prod_{i=1}^k\Z/p_i$, with the $p_i$ odd primes, has a selector with $2^k$ values. In $\Z/9$, the elements $0,3,6$ all square to $0$, so every selector needs at least three values.

\section{Order structure}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:order}
If $2\in R^\times$, then $0\le\sumH\le\hV$, $0\le\gapH\le\hV$, and $\syn\ge-\min(\sumH,\gapH)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The hinted views refine $N$. The joint view refines both hinted views because $N=(s^2-d^2)/4$. Apply monotonicity. For the last claim, if $\sumH\le\gapH$ then $\syn=(\hV-\gapH)-\sumH\ge-\sumH$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
If $T$ is a function of $N$, then all hints vanish and $\syn=0$.
\end{proposition}

\section{The quadratic bottleneck and the One-Bit Law}

\begin{lemma}[Refinement ceiling]\label{lem:refine}
Let $f$ refine $g$, and let $e:\mathcal A\to F$, with $F$ finite, be such that $g(x)=g(y)$ and $e(f(x))=e(f(y))$ imply $f(x)=f(y)$. Then $I(T;f)\le I(T;g)+\log_2|F|$ for every label $T$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The statistics $f$ and $(g,e\circ f)$ have the same fibres, so
$I(T;f)=I(T;g,e\circ f)\le I(T;g)+H(e\circ f)\le I(T;g)+\log_2|F|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Quadratic identity]\label{lem:quad}
In every commutative ring, $d^2=s^2-4N$ and $s^2=d^2+4N$. Hence samples with equal $(N,s)$ have gaps with equal squares, and samples with equal $(N,d)$ have sums with equal squares.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$\log_2|F|$ law]\label{thm:logF}
Let $2\in R^\times$ and let $\sigma:R\to F$ be a sign selector. For every battery over $R$,
\[
\hV\le\sumH+\log_2|F|,\qquad \hV\le\gapH+\log_2|F|,
\]
and therefore
\[
\syn\le\log_2|F|-\max(\sumH,\gapH)\le\log_2|F|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:refine} with $f=(s,d)$, $g=(N,s)$ and $e(s,d)=\sigma(d)$. Refinement holds since $N=(s^2-d^2)/4$. If $g(x)=g(y)$ and $\sigma(d(x))=\sigma(d(y))$, Lemma~\ref{lem:quad} gives $d(x)^2=d(y)^2$, so $d(x)=d(y)$. Subtracting $I(T;N)$ gives the first inequality. The gap form uses $e(s,d)=\sigma(s)$. For the synergy, if $\sumH\le\gapH$ then $\syn=(\hV-\sumH)-\gapH\le\log_2|F|-\gapH$. The other case is symmetric. The final inequality uses non-negativity of the hints.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The One-Bit Law]\label{cor:onebit}
For an odd prime $p$ and any battery over $\Z/p$,
$\syn\le1-\max(\sumH,\gapH)\le1$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:logF} with Lemma~\ref{lem:zsel}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[All-or-nothing]\label{cor:aon}
Under the hypotheses of Corollary~\ref{cor:onebit}, $\syn=1$ if and only if $\sumH=\gapH=0$ and $\hV=1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[One orientation bit per field]\label{thm:kfield}
Let $p_1,\dots,p_k$ be odd primes and $R=\prod_{i=1}^k\Z/p_i$. For every battery over $R$, $\syn\le k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemmas~\ref{lem:zsel} and~\ref{lem:prod} give a selector with $2^k$ values. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:logF}.
\end{proof}

\section{Sharpness}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{example}[Redundant battery, synergy $-1$]
Over $\Z/5$, take samples $(p,q)=(1,1)$ with label $0$ and $(2,3)$ with label $1$. Both have $N=1$, their sums are $2,0$ and their gaps are $0,1$. Each of $(N,s)$, $(N,d)$ and $(s,d)$ separates the samples, so $\sumH=\gapH=\hV=1$ and $\syn=-1$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Compounding battery, synergy $+1$]
Over $\Z/7$, take the four samples of Table~\ref{tab:c7} on $pq\equiv1$.
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{cccccc}
\toprule
sample & $(p,q)$ & $T$ & $N$ & $s$ & $d$\\
\midrule
$x_0$ & $(2,4)$ & $0$ & $1$ & $6$ & $2$\\
$x_1$ & $(4,2)$ & $1$ & $1$ & $6$ & $5$\\
$x_2$ & $(3,5)$ & $1$ & $1$ & $1$ & $2$\\
$x_3$ & $(5,3)$ & $0$ & $1$ & $1$ & $5$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The mod-$7$ compounding battery.}\label{tab:c7}
\end{table}
Each fibre of $(N,s)$, namely $\{x_0,x_1\}$ and $\{x_2,x_3\}$, and each fibre of $(N,d)$, namely $\{x_0,x_2\}$ and $\{x_1,x_3\}$, contains one sample of each label. So $\sumH=\gapH=0$. The joint view separates all samples, so $\hV=1$ and $\syn=+1$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}
Hint synergy has no sign, and the bound of Corollary~\ref{cor:onebit} is sharp.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Two-field battery, synergy $+2$]
Over $\Z/7\times\Z/7$, take the $16$-sample product of two copies of the previous battery, with label $T=2T_i+T_j$. Fibre counts give $H(T)=2$, $I(T;N)=I(T;N,s)=I(T;N,d)=0$ and $I(T;s,d)=2$, so $\sumH=\gapH=0$, $\hV=2$ and $\syn=2$.
\end{example}

\begin{corollary}
A conditional hint synergy of $1.4$ bits is impossible over a single odd prime field and is realised over $\Z/7\times\Z/7$.
\end{corollary}

The reported $+1.40$ bits was measured on a two-prime modulus, which is the case $k=2$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:kfield}. It is not evidence of unbounded compounding. The experimental per-dial rows need not coincide with the conditional hints defined here.

\section{The orientation bit is a quadratic residue}\label{sec:legendre}

A \emph{cell} is a pair $(n,\sigma)$ with discriminant $\Delta=\sigma^2-4n$. A \emph{factorisation} of the cell is an $a$ with $a(\sigma-a)=n$.

\begin{lemma}[Change of variable]\label{lem:cov}
In a field $K$ with $2\ne0$: $a(\sigma-a)=n\iff(2a-\sigma)^2=\sigma^2-4n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(2a-\sigma)^2-(\sigma^2-4n)=-4\,(a(\sigma-a)-n)$, and $4\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:twoRoots}
In a field $K$ with $2\neq0$, the cell $(n,\sigma)$ has two distinct factorisations if and only if some $t\neq0$ satisfies $t^2=\sigma^2-4n$. If $\Delta\neq0$, this holds if and only if $\Delta$ is a square.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Given $a\ne b$, put $t=2a-\sigma$. If $t=0$, then $\Delta=0$, so $(2b-\sigma)^2=0$, which forces $2b=2a$ and $a=b$. Conversely, $a,b=(\sigma\pm t)/2$ are factorisations by Lemma~\ref{lem:cov}, and $a-b=t\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Legendre Law for Orientation]\label{thm:legendre}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $n,\sigma\in\Z/p$ with $\Delta=\sigma^2-4n\neq0$. The cell $(n,\sigma)$ hosts two distinct factorisations, and hence an orientation bit, if and only if $\leg{\Delta}{p}=1$. In general the number of factorisations is $1+\leg{\sigma^2-4n}{p}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}
Modulo $7$, the cell $(1,6)$ has $\Delta=32\equiv4=2^2$ and hosts $(2,4)$ and $(4,2)$. The cell $(1,0)$ has $\Delta\equiv3$, a non-residue, and hosts no factorisation.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[Cell census]
For fixed $\sigma$, the map $n\mapsto\sigma^2-4n$ is a bijection of $\Z/p$. So exactly $p(p-1)/2$ of the $p^2$ cells are residue cells, $p$ are zero cells and $p(p-1)/2$ are non-residue cells. For $p=7$ this is $21$, $7$ and $21$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[No orientation, no compounding]\label{thm:noorient}
If $2\in R^\times$ and $(N,s)$ determines $d$ on the battery, then $\hV=\sumH$ and $\syn=-\gapH\le0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Under the hypothesis, $(s,d)$ and $(N,s)$ have the same fibres. Apply fibre invariance.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A battery with $P=Q$ throughout has $\syn\le0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Positive orientation gain needs a residue cell]
If $\sumH<\hV$, some sample has $d\neq0$, and its cell has discriminant $d^2$, a nonzero square.
\end{theorem}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Exact routing table.} Compute $N,s,d$ componentwise. Tabulate the counts of $T$, $N$, $(N,s)$, $(N,d)$, $(s,d)$ and their pairings with $T$. Evaluate $H$ and $I$ from the counts. This takes $O(|\Omega|)$ time with hashing.

\textbf{Orientation-cell classification.} Compute $\Delta=\sigma^2-4n$ and $\chi=\Delta^{(p-1)/2}\bmod p$. If $\Delta=0$, there is one factorisation $(\sigma/2,\sigma/2)$. If $\chi=1$, find $t$ with $t^2=\Delta$ by Tonelli--Shanks and return $((\sigma\pm t)/2,(\sigma\mp t)/2)$. Otherwise there is no factorisation. The cost is $O(\log p)$ multiplications for the symbol and expected $O(\log^2p)$ for the root.

\section{Discussion}

Capacity synergy, the unconditional interaction information of residue views, is bounded only by label entropy. Conditional hint synergy is bounded by the size of the sign group $\{\pm1\}^k$ of the modulus. The difference comes from the algebraic coupling $d^2=s^2-4N$. Over the integers, $(N,s)$ recovers $\{p,q\}$ via Fermat's identity $N=(s/2)^2-(d/2)^2$. Modulo primes, the ordered pair costs exactly one extra bit per field, available precisely on residue cells.

The experiment also reported a which-factor statistic of $0.9663$ bits in the $(s,d)$ view. It was estimated on about $508{,}000$ cells from $30{,}000$ samples, which is the extreme sparse plug-in regime. We do not interpret it, and none of the results above depend on it. Since $s$ and $d^2$ are invariant under $p\leftrightarrow q$, genuine leakage would be orientation-conditional. A permutation-null test is the next step.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \emph{Square-root multiplicity ceiling:} for finite $R$ with $2\in R^\times$, is the supremal synergy equal to $\log_2\max_e|\{d:d^2=e\}|$? For $\Z/9$ this predicts $\log_2 3\approx1.585$ bits.
\item \emph{Tensor batteries:} does the $k$-fold product of the mod-$7$ battery attain $k$ bits for all $k$?
\item \emph{Population-weighted density} of residue cells, via character sums.
\item A permutation-null test of the flagged which-factor statistic.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Over one odd prime field, the sum and gap dials together exceed their separate conditional values by at most one bit, and this is attained only when both are individually worthless. Over $k$ fields the ceiling is $k$ bits, attained for $k=2$. The bit is the orientation of the factor pair, the sign of the root in $d^2=s^2-4N$. It exists in a cell exactly when $\leg{s^2-4N}{p}=1$.

